WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.760
Is it the force, deepfakes or fake babies?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:09.544
A lot goes into making The Mandalorian look&nbsp;
like it takes place in a galaxy far far away.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:14.240
Every installment in the Star Wars empire&nbsp;
features new creatures from far-off planets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:18.160
that only the most creative minds could&nbsp;
dream up. This is true even in the early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:23.120
episodes of The Mandalorian. Consider the&nbsp;
Blurrgs, for instance. These fat, bipedal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:27.920
possibly reptilian creatures have been&nbsp;
seen running around on Arvala-7. Din Djarin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:32.880
finds himself on this planet during one of his&nbsp;
missions and uses these creatures to get around.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:36.400
Pixomondo's visual effects&nbsp;
supervisor Goran Backman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:39.200
explained to DigitalTrends some&nbsp;
of the challenges they had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:43.520
overcome to make the Blurrgs look as&nbsp;
realistic as possible. As he put it,

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:47.120
"Since we had a lot of interaction&nbsp;
between the actors and the creatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:50.960
we also had to drive the on-set, practical&nbsp;
buck — that's what we called the saddle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:54.480
that the actors would ride, sort of&nbsp;
like the seat on a mechanical bull."

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:59.040
The actors' riding motions were captured with&nbsp;
mechanical pistons at the base of the practical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:03.680
Blurrgs. Then after filming, special effects&nbsp;
teams created the Blurrgs' remaining body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:08.400
stationary, and in-motion poses. And&nbsp;
interestingly enough, the Pixomondo team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.720
often referenced rhinos and elephants&nbsp;
for some of the Blurrgs' features.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:17.760
Grogu, more lovingly known as Baby Yoda,&nbsp;
is arguably The Mandalorian's true star.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:22.720
This cute little green fellow spends his time on&nbsp;
the show trailing after Din as the two venture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:27.520
around the Outer Rim. He's often sitting&nbsp;
comfortably in his floating egg-shaped pram,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:31.760
but no matter what he's doing, it takes&nbsp;
an entire team to bring him to life.

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:37.040
This tiny alien was created through the combined&nbsp;
efforts of animatronics and CGI technology.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:40.480
According to the docu-series&nbsp;
Disney Gallery: The Mandalorian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:45.440
it took three months to develop and build the&nbsp;
Grogu that ended up on screen. Behind the camera,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:51.680
it took one person each to puppeteer the arms,&nbsp;
eyes, body, head, ears, and walking movements.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:57.120
The use of CGI only came into play to remove&nbsp;
the wires and poles used to control Grogu.

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:01.040
Bryce Dallas Howard, who has directed&nbsp;
a couple of episodes, was especially in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:06.480
favor of using animatronic creatures instead of&nbsp;
exclusively CGI technology. According to her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:12.000
there's a special magic that comes from using a&nbsp;
moveable prop instead of solely utilizing CGI.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.560
The actors' reactions to seeing Baby Yoda&nbsp;
were authentic, and the physical acting and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:20.880
movements with Grogu were far more real&nbsp;
thanks to being captured in real time.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:23.536
"You get to have the experience, like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:27.597
live of what the audience gets to&nbsp;
experience when they're watching a movie."

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:31.760
The minds behind The Mandalorian have been&nbsp;
widely praised for their unique ways of bringing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:36.640
this sci-fi world to life. That's certainly&nbsp;
clear in the construction of Din's spaceship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:40.480
the Razor Crest. Instead of opting&nbsp;
for computer graphics, the visual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:45.440
effects creators from Industrial Light &amp; Magic&nbsp;
worked on miniature models of the spacecraft.

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:49.040
3D printers were used to build&nbsp;
the Razor Crest piece by piece.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:54.080
John Goodson, the physical model maker from&nbsp;
ILM, then put the individual pieces together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:58.160
added the ship's exterior layers, and&nbsp;
took care of the fine paint details.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:03.088
In order to get the ship to the big screen, a&nbsp;
specially designed camera rig filmed the model.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.088 --> 00:03:07.520
As the ship sat in one spot with a surrounding&nbsp;
bluescreen, the rigged camera flew past.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:11.440
And to give the craft more movement,&nbsp;
techs wobbled the model from side to side.

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:15.600
This technique of filming a model was a&nbsp;
bit of an homage to the original trilogy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:18.720
as practical miniature ships&nbsp;
of that era were also built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:23.101
and shot in front of blue screens and&nbsp;
then later added to starry backgrounds.

00:03:23.276 --> 00:03:27.360
The use of a Darksaber by the villainous&nbsp;
Moff Gideon posed a unique challenge to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:32.400
The Mandalorian's prop designers. Prop sabers&nbsp;
were continuously being redesigned and built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:37.360
in order to get Gideon's weapon to look as&nbsp;
accurate as possible. Unlike standard lightsabers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:42.400
the Darksabers' history had only ever been&nbsp;
mentioned in The Clone Wars and Star Wars: Rebels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:46.320
and it had only appeared as a cartoon&nbsp;
instead of in a physical form. Thus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:50.160
it was difficult to get this new&nbsp;
weapon exactly right. As Hal Hickel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:54.720
The Mandalorian's animation supervisor,&nbsp;
explained in Disney Gallery: The Mandalorian:

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:58.000
"There was a lot of time put into getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:01.200
the energy effect just right&nbsp;
that you see along the blade."

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:05.680
The physical prop that was used during filming&nbsp;
was lit to give the surrounding area the lighting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:10.480
effect that would normally come through from using&nbsp;
a lighted weapon. The rest of the warbling effects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:15.360
that are seen during the final battle scene were&nbsp;
the result of many hours of digital rendering.

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:19.600
Lesser showrunners might have backed down from&nbsp;
using something of the Darksaber's caliber.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:23.120
But the special effects team of The&nbsp;
Mandalorian rose to the challenge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:28.635
and created an actual physical sword that Star&nbsp;
Wars fans have been anticipating for a long time.

00:04:28.741 --> 00:04:34.480
When it came time to bring IG-11 to life as a&nbsp;
robotic bounty hunter, visual effects animator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:40.240
Hal Hickel recognized the distinct differences&nbsp;
between this droid and K-2SO from Rogue One:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:45.920
A Star Wars Story. K-2SO was designed to&nbsp;
be as close to human as a robot could get.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:50.160
By using motion capture suits, those&nbsp;
smoother motions were easily achieved.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:54.960
But in the case of IG-11, the special effects&nbsp;
team resisted the use of motion capture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.960 --> 00:05:00.560
and instead used a pre-built statue-like structure&nbsp;
that had intentional awkwardness in its movements.

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:04.960
While there were a few parts, like the head,&nbsp;
that could be moved remotely from a distance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:09.920
the rest of the body had to be manually&nbsp;
arranged behind the scenes. This gave IG-11 a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:15.600
rickety effect and thereby created an older, more&nbsp;
rugged look that's perfect for a space Western.

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:18.560
"It was exactly the right awkwardness."

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:23.120
The Mandalorian creator Jon Favreau is never&nbsp;
one to shy away from a cinematic challenge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:26.160
He learned some valuable lessons&nbsp;
in behind-the-scenes technology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:31.760
during his time directing both 2016's The&nbsp;
Jungle Book and 2019's The Lion King. Many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:36.400
of the problems encountered during production of&nbsp;
those films came from the use of excessive blue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:41.680
and green screens and the lack of interactive&nbsp;
sets around real-life actors. So the solution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:46.640
was to invent revolutionary set technology.&nbsp;
As Favreau explained on Disney Gallery,

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:52.080
"The Mandalorian is the first production ever to&nbsp;
use real-time rendering and video wall in-camera,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:56.400
set extensions, and effects. [...] Necessity&nbsp;
was the mother of invention because we were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:59.600
trying to figure out how to do the&nbsp;
production here in the timeframe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:02.960
at the budget level, but still get the&nbsp;
whole look that we're used to seeing."

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:07.040
"What did we learn on Lion King and Jungle&nbsp;
Book? How do you apply it to The Mandalorian?"

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:11.440
Massive LED screens projected the scene's&nbsp;
background to create the effect that actors and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:16.880
camera crews were filming at outdoor locations.&nbsp;
By utilizing positional data from cameras,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:22.320
the high-tech backgrounds can change as the camera&nbsp;
moves. The result creates perspective or parallax,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:26.640
which can be defined by an object appearing&nbsp;
slightly different from two different angles of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:31.920
view. This gives viewers a more realistic feeling&nbsp;
of being outside and allows actors to feel like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:37.064
they're actually in a far-off world, something&nbsp;
traditional green and blue screens can't do.

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:40.720
Practical effects were a critical&nbsp;
part of creating The Mandalorian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:45.840
including the animatronics of Baby Yoda and&nbsp;
the remote-controlled approach of IG-11.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:50.640
But while these techniques are effective to an&nbsp;
extent, if you don't have an actor behind a mask,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:55.040
that takes away some of the personality of&nbsp;
certain characters. A solution for this is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.040 --> 00:07:00.480
use of animatronic prosthetics. This combination&nbsp;
of humanity and technology is really fundamental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:05.360
to Star Wars. As director Rick Famuyiwa&nbsp;
put it on Disney Gallery: The Mandalorian,

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:09.840
"One part doesn't dominate the other, doesn't&nbsp;
feel like the technology is overwhelming."

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:10.000
"It doesn't feel like, 'Well, that's quaint and&nbsp;
primitive, so, you know, I'm not buying it.'"

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:16.000
Consider the character Kuiil, an Ugnaught voiced&nbsp;
by Nick Nolte and played by Misty Rosas. According&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:21.120
to the creators who developed these prosthetics,&nbsp;
Rosas' acting ability gave Kuiil the mobility that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:26.000
allowed the character a softer and more relatable&nbsp;
persona while the animatronic mask created a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:31.120
face that viewers hadn't seen since The Empire&nbsp;
Strikes Back. Similar combinations of animatronic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:36.240
prosthetics and actors were seen again with the&nbsp;
Frog Lady in Season 2. Viewers were able to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:41.508
a unique blend of humanity and alien emotion&nbsp;
that wouldn't have otherwise been achievable.

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:44.720
When Jon Favreau broke the news&nbsp;
of Luke Skywalker's appearance in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:49.680
The Mandalorian's Season 2 finale, the&nbsp;
atmosphere on set changed drastically.

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:53.040
Disney Gallery: The Mandalorian&nbsp;
discussed the de-aging process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:56.880
that was used to get Mark Hamill’s&nbsp;
present day face back to its circa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:02.240
1983 look. The actor admitted that he felt&nbsp;
surprised by the proposal to play Luke again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:06.320
but ultimately came to the conclusion that&nbsp;
he couldn't say no to the opportunity.

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:10.640
The process that best suited the needs of The&nbsp;
Mandalorian was to replace Hamill's real face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:16.240
with an AI -generated image of his younger face&nbsp;
using a deepfake program. In order to create this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:22.400
younger visage, art director Landis Fields took 4K&nbsp;
footage from all three original Star Wars films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:27.120
interviews of Hamill from the same time period,&nbsp;
and still photographs from the set to compile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:31.920
what Favreau calls the "library of faces." With&nbsp;
thousands of images of Luke from the era of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:37.120
the original trilogy, the deepfake program's AI&nbsp;
could then rebuild expressions and match facial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:42.080
characteristics and specific movements that&nbsp;
line up with Hamill's lines in The Mandalorian.

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:46.400
That “library of faces” was then added&nbsp;
onto an actor – and there you have it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:49.446
a 1983-era Mark Hamill!

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:53.920
A major goal of The Mandalorian showrunners&nbsp;
has been to give audiences more than a simple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:58.960
illusion of reality. When it comes to filming&nbsp;
varying geographies of foreign planets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:02.800
creators of the series have taken it upon&nbsp;
themselves to film as though they're actually in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:08.240
unfamiliar conditions. For example, when it came&nbsp;
time to create the planet of Trask in Season 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:10.560
director Bryce Dallas Howard explained,

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:14.240
"You can't just say, 'Oh, the vision effects&nbsp;
are going to make it look real.' You want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:17.920
to shoot it in a manner in which you would&nbsp;
actually have to shoot it if it was real."

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:21.600
"And then the audience believes that it's real."

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:26.480
Of course, digital special effects, CGI,&nbsp;
and other combined computerized renderings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:30.960
are the cherries that top the cake. But in order&nbsp;
to achieve the look that made up most of the boat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:35.760
scenes in this episode, a massive set was built&nbsp;
to replicate the ship's deck. The challenge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:40.800
according to the special effects crew, was making&nbsp;
a static boat stage look like a moving ship.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:45.200
Of course, with the use of those fancy LED&nbsp;
landscape screens and the ability to alter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:49.600
parallax views, as well as specialized&nbsp;
camera movements and CGI additions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.600 --> 00:09:53.040
the boat set ultimately&nbsp;
never had to actually move.

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:57.440
True lightsaber visuals have always been&nbsp;
a visual effects feature exclusive to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.440 --> 00:10:02.720
Star Wars franchise. And the weapon's inclusion&nbsp;
in The Mandalorian is particularly special. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:07.200
the same way that Moff Gideon's Darksaber made&nbsp;
its first physical appearance on the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:11.040
Ahsoka Tano's twin white blades made&nbsp;
their live-action debut as well.

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:16.000
Being able to capture actual light from props was&nbsp;
a relatively new way to film lightsabers when The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:21.200
Mandalorian was filmed. To get the right effect&nbsp;
of lightsabers during filming, a visual technique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:26.800
known as interactive lighting was utilized. This&nbsp;
process involved LED lights built into the base of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:31.840
prop sabers, which allowed the camera to capture&nbsp;
how sabers would project real light and shadows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:37.120
on and around the actors without obscuring the&nbsp;
camera's field of vision with light distortion.

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:42.240
From there, cinematographers were easily able to&nbsp;
frame the camera on specific focal points based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:46.800
on where the interactive lighting from the prop&nbsp;
lightsabers were highlighting character features.

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:51.360
Without the light source in the props, early&nbsp;
film lightsabers didn't cast light or shadows.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:56.400
This was noticeable by the lack of lightsaber&nbsp;
glow reflected on actors' faces. Sabers from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.400 --> 00:11:00.720
original Star Wars trilogy got their glow&nbsp;
effect from reflective tape on the props.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:04.560
Colors and other effects were added to&nbsp;
individual frames in post-production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:10.000
using a technique known as rotoscoping. When you&nbsp;
compare that to The Mandalorian, you realize just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:14.566
how far the worlds of special effects and&nbsp;
Star Wars have come in the decades since.

